{
  "teamId": 141,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Toronto Blue Jays season was a slow, painful unraveling of a franchise that had been built for October contention. Opening day brought a flash of promise — Davis Schneider's two-run blast on April 2nd gave the Blue Jays one of their most impactful individual moments of the entire year, a +0.731 WPA swing that suggested the young outfielder could be a difference-maker. But that early joy would prove to be an outlier in a season defined more by collapse than by clutch.\n\nMay became a month of mounting damage. Jordan Romano, once the closer Toronto could count on, twice surrendered crushing home runs in high-leverage situations. Alek Manoah, a former ace, gave up a three-run shot that symbolized his continued struggles. Nate Pearson and Erik Swanson added their own damaging appearances, and even a Daulton Varsho walk-off three-run homer on May 26th couldn't paper over the cracks — that same day, the bullpen imploded twice more, surrendering late leads and turning a potential emotional boost into a net negative.\n\nThe summer months offered occasional glimmers. Ernie Clement delivered a surprise three-run blast in July, Alejandro Kirk lifted spirits with a go-ahead home run in August, and young outfielder Joey Loperfido showed why the future might hold some promise. But for every bright moment there was a Trevor Richards meltdown, a Chad Green disaster, or a Kevin Gausman grand slam surrender to dim the lights again. By September, with Chad Green allowing Royce Lewis a review-confirmed homer in a -0.605 WPA gut-punch and Kyle Schwarber adding insult to injury days later, the season had fully caved in on itself.\n\nThe Blue Jays finished 74-88, missing the playoffs and watching their run differential balloon to -72. The 2024 campaign became less a story of a team fighting until the end and more an honest reckoning — with an aging core, a broken bullpen, and a roster caught between contention and rebuild. Spencer Horwitz's late-season homer and some encouraging showings from youngsters like Addison Barger offered small consolations, but the clearest takeaway was that Toronto would need significant reinvention to compete in 2025.",
